oints outside scratch_root and is later modified, the race is exploitable. Additionally, .resolve() can throw FileNotFoundError for non-existent paths, which may bypass validation if not caught.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Use .resolve(strict=False) to avoid FileNotFoundError on non-existent paths. Check containment before AND after resolution. For symlinks, verify the symlink target itself is within scratch_root before following. Add tests for: symlink -> /etc/passwd, symlink -> ../../../outside, non-existent path with .., broken symlink.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/isolation.py:200\",\n        \"tests/cli/eval/test_isolation.py:1\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"AC12 scratch-root allowlist tautology fix (commit dce3c3cb) may still allow unintended paths\",\n      \"category\": \"security\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"high\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/isolation.py\",\n      \"line\": 95,\n      \"line_range_end\": 115,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"def _is_path_allowed(self, path: Path) -> bool:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Check if path is within scratch_root or explicitly allowed.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    if self._is_within(path, self._scratch_root):\\n        return True\\n    for allowed in self._allowed_paths:\\n        if self._is_within(path, allowed):\\n            return True\\n    return False\",\n      \"why\": \"Commit dce3c3cb fixed a tautology but the allowlist logic still lacks validation that allowed_paths entries themselves are safe. If an allowed_path is /tmp and scratch_root is /eval/scratch, then /tmp/malicious is allowed. The default-deny posture relies on the caller passing a safe allowlist, not on validation within isolation.py.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Add validation in __init__ that rejects allowed_paths entries that overlap with sensitive system directories (/etc, /usr, /var, /sys, /proc, /dev, /home). Document that allowed_paths is a trusted input. Add tests for: allowed_paths=['/etc'] (should reject), allowed_paths=['/tmp'] (should warn or reject unless explicitly needed).\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/isolation.py:45\",\n        \"tests/cli/eval/test_isolation.py:1\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Reporter.py violates FR-G1 ban-import rule: imports from superclaude.core\",\n      \"category\": \"layering\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"critical\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/reporter.py\",\n      \"line\": 8,\n      \"line_range_end\": 12,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"from superclaude.core.logging import get_logger\\nfrom superclaude.core.config import get_config\",\n      \"why\": \"FR-G1 explicitly bans eval harness modules from importing superclaude core/agents/skills to maintain isolation and prevent circular dependencies. reporter.py imports superclaude.core.logging and superclaude.core.config, violating this contract. This creates coupling that breaks the eval harness's ability to run standalone.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Replace superclaude.core.logging with stdlib logging. Replace superclaude.core.config with direct access to eval config objects passed as parameters. Add a lint rule (ruff or custom script) to enforce FR-G1 ban across src/superclaude/cli/eval/.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/commands.py:1\",\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/runner.py:1\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Broad except clause swallows all exceptions in runner.py run_spec\",\n      \"category\": \"error-handling\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/runner.py\",\n      \"line\": 245,\n      \"line_range_end\": 255,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"try:\\n    result = await self._execute_spec(spec)\\n    return result\\nexcept Exception as e:\\n    logger.error(f\\\"Spec {spec.name} failed: {e}\\\")\\n    return SpecResult(\\n        name=spec.name,\\n        status=SpecStatus.FAILED,\\n        error=str(e)\\n    )\",\n      \"why\": \"The broad except Exception catches all exceptions including KeyboardInterrupt (in Python 2) and SystemExit, though Python 3 makes these BaseException. Still, catching Exception masks bugs: if _execute_spec has a logic error (AttributeError, TypeError), it's logged but not surfaced to the caller, making debugging harder.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Catch specific exceptions expected from _execute_spec (e.g., SpecExecutionError, TimeoutError, subprocess.CalledProcessError). Let unexpected exceptions propagate to fail fast. If broad catch is needed for robustness, re-raise after logging if the exception is unexpected (isinstance check).\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/runner.py:300\",\n        \"tests/cli/eval/test_runner.py:1\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Magic number 130 hardcoded for SIGINT exit code without explanation\",\n      \"category\": \"style\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"low\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/commands.py\",\n      \"line\": 33,\n      \"line_range_end\": 33,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"RUN_INTERRUPTED_EXIT_CODE: Final[int] = 130\",\n      \"why\": \"The constant 130 is the conventional exit code for SIGINT (128 + signal number 2) but this is not documented. A reader unfamiliar with POSIX signal conventions won't understand why 130 specifically.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Add a comment: # 130 = 128 + SIGINT (signal 2), POSIX convention for script terminated by signal. Alternatively, compute it: RUN_INTERRUPTED_EXIT_CODE: Final[int] = 128 + signal.SIGINT.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/signal_handler.py:1\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Disk budget logic in disk_budget.py uses float arithmetic for byte counts, risking precision loss\",\n      \"category\": \"correctness\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/disk_budget.py\",\n      \"line\": 180,\n      \"line_range_end\": 195,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"def _calculate_budget_fraction(used_bytes: int, limit_bytes: int) -> float:\\n    if limit_bytes == 0:\\n        return 0.0\\n    return used_bytes / limit_bytes\\n\\ndef _check_budget(self, used_bytes: int) -> None:\\n    fraction = self._calculate_budget_fraction(used_bytes, self._limit_bytes)\\n    if fraction > 1.0:\\n        raise DiskBudgetExceeded(...)\",\n      \"why\": \"Dividing int byte counts produces float, which can lose precision for very large values (>2^53). For disk usage in the GB-TB range, this is unlikely to matter, but it's fragile. Checking fraction > 1.0 is safe, but logging fraction as percentage may show misleading values if float precision is lost.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Use integer comparison: if used_bytes > self._limit_bytes: raise DiskBudgetExceeded(...). For logging percentage, compute int((used_bytes * 100) // limit_bytes) to stay in integer domain. Only convert to float for display formatting.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/disk_budget.py:220\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Missing type hints on several public functions in expect.py\",\n      \"category\": \"quality\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"low\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/expect.py\",\n      \"line\": 105,\n      \"line_range_end\": 120,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"def expect_file_contains(path, pattern, timeout=10):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Wait for file to contain pattern.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    ...\\n\\ndef expect_exit_code(process, expected_code):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Assert process exited with expected code.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    ...\",\n      \"why\": \"expect.py provides primitives used across tests but several functions lack type hints (path, pattern, timeout, process, expected_code). This reduces IDE autocomplete quality and makes refactoring risky. The module is 722 LOC; missing type hints on public API is a quality gap.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Add type hints to all public functions in expect.py. Use Path for path, str|re.Pattern for pattern, float for timeout, subprocess.Popen for process, int for expected_code. Run mypy --strict on the module to catch remaining gaps.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/expect.py:200\",\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/expect.py:350\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Potential TOCTOU race in coverage.py: file modification between stat and read\",\n      \"category\": \"concurrency\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"low\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/coverage.py\",\n      \"line\": 210,\n      \"line_range_end\": 225,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"def _read_coverage_file(self, path: Path) -> dict:\\n    if not path.exists():\\n        raise CoverageFileNotFound(path)\\n    stat = path.stat()\\n    if stat.st_size > self._max_file_size:\\n        raise CoverageFileTooLarge(path, stat.st_size)\\n    with path.open('r') as f:\\n        return json.load(f)\",\n      \"why\": \"Between path.exists(), path.stat(), and path.open(), another process could delete, replace, or modify the file. In an eval harness running concurrent specs, this is unlikely but possible. The file could grow beyond _max_file_size between stat and open, bypassing the check.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Use try/except FileNotFoundError around open to handle deletion. For size check, read into BytesIO with a max-size limit (io.BytesIO(f.read(max_file_size))) or use a streaming JSON parser with size cap. Accept that TOCTOU is unavoidable on filesystem and focus on graceful error handling.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/coverage.py:240\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"God class: EvalConfig in models.py holds 30+ fields with minimal encapsulation\",\n      \"category\": \"anti-pattern\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/models.py\",\n      \"line\": 45,\n      \"line_range_end\": 150,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"@dataclass\\nclass EvalConfig:\\n    suite_path: Path\\n    output_dir: Path\\n    scratch_root: Path\\n    timeout: int\\n    parallelism: int\\n    retry_limit: int\\n    disk_budget_mb: int\\n    coverage_threshold: float\\n    # ... 20+ more fields\",\n      \"why\": \"EvalConfig is a dataclass with 30+ fields spanning concerns: paths, timeouts, parallelism, disk budget, coverage, hooks, retry, isolation. This is primitive obsession and god class: every module imports EvalConfig and accesses arbitrary fields, creating high coupling. Changes to config structure ripple everywhere.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Split EvalConfig into domain-specific configs: PathConfig (suite_path, output_dir, scratch_root), ExecutionConfig (timeout, parallelism, retry_limit), ResourceConfig (disk_budget_mb), CoverageConfig (coverage_threshold, coverage_files). Use composition: EvalConfig wraps these. Update modules to depend on specific sub-configs.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/commands.py:1\",\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/runner.py:1\",\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/orchestrator.py:1\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Unused import: typing.Optional not used in artifact_layout.py\",\n      \"category\": \"quality\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"low\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/artifact_layout.py\",\n      \"line\": 3,\n      \"line_range_end\": 3,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"from typing import Optional, Dict, List\",\n      \"why\": \"Commit 08183738 cleaned up F401 unused imports but artifact_layout.py still imports Optional without using it (checked via grep). This is a linting violation (F401) and adds noise.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Remove Optional from the import line. Run ruff check --select F401 src/superclaude/cli/eval/ to catch remaining unused imports.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"PTY driver in pty_driver.py lacks timeout enforcement on read operations\",\n      \"category\": \"resource-leak\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/pty_driver.py\",\n      \"line\": 180,\n      \"line_range_end\": 200,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"def read_output(self, max_bytes: int = 4096) -> bytes:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Read output from PTY, blocking until data available.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    if self._fd is None:\\n        raise PTYNotOpen()\\n    return os.read(self._fd, max_bytes)\",\n      \"why\": \"os.read on a PTY fd blocks indefinitely if no data is available. If the child process hangs without producing output, read_output will block forever, leaking resources. The eval harness spec timeout may not kill this read if it's in a separate thread.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Use select.select([self._fd], [], [], timeout) to implement read with timeout. Raise PTYReadTimeout if select times out. Ensure all read_output call sites handle timeout gracefully. Add tests for: child hangs without output, child exits without flushing output.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/pty_driver.py:220\",\n        \"tests/cli/eval/test_pty_driver.py:1\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Signal handler installs globally in signal_handler.py but does not restore original handler on cleanup\",\n      \"category\": \"correctness\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/signal_handler.py\",\n      \"line\": 55,\n      \"line_range_end\": 70,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"def install_signal_handler(self, signal_num: int) -> None:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Install handler for signal.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    signal.signal(signal_num, self._handle_signal)\\n\\ndef cleanup(self) -> None:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Cleanup handler state.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    self._interrupted = False\\n    # Does not restore original signal handler\",\n      \"why\": \"install_signal_handler replaces the existing signal handler but does not save the original. cleanup() resets internal state but leaves the custom handler installed. If the eval harness is embedded in a larger application, this can break the host's signal handling.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"In install_signal_handler, save the original handler: self._original_handlers[signal_num] = signal.signal(signal_num, self._handle_signal). In cleanup(), restore: signal.signal(signal_num, self._original_handlers[signal_num]). Use a context manager for install/cleanup lifecycle.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/commands.py:150\",\n        \"tests/cli/eval/test_signal_handler.py:1\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Loader.py loads suite schema but does not validate spec-level schema constraints\",\n      \"category\": \"data-integrity\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/loader.py\",\n      \"line\": 120,\n      \"line_range_end\": 145,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"def _load_suite(self, suite_path: Path) -> Suite:\\n    with suite_path.open('r') as f:\\n        data = json.load(f)\\n    # Validate against suite.schema.json\\n    jsonschema.validate(data, self._suite_schema)\\n    # Does not validate individual spec schemas\",\n      \"why\": \"The loader validates the suite JSON against suite.schema.json but does not validate individual spec files against their own schemas (if they exist). If a spec references a malformed expect file or has invalid timeout=-1, the error surfaces late during execution, not at load time.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Add spec-level schema validation in _load_spec(). Define a spec.schema.json if one doesn't exist. Validate expect files, timeout bounds, required fields. Fail fast at load time with clear error messages pointing to the invalid spec file.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/schemas/suite.schema.json:1\",\n        \"tests/cli/eval/test_loader.py:1\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Retry logic in retry.py does not implement exponential backoff or jitter\",\n      \"category\": \"performance\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"low\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/retry.py\",\n      \"line\": 45,\n      \"line_range_end\": 65,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"def retry_with_delay(func, max_retries: int, delay: float):\\n    for attempt in range(max_retries):\\n        try:\\n            return func()\\n        except Exception as e:\\n            if attempt == max_retries - 1:\\n                raise\\n            time.sleep(delay)  # Fixed delay, no backoff\",\n      \"why\": \"Fixed delay retry is fragile under load: if all specs retry simultaneously (e.g., shared resource contention), they hammer the resource at the same cadence. Exponential backoff with jitter spreads retries over time, reducing thundering herd risk.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Implement exponential backoff: delay = base_delay * (2 ** attempt). Add jitter: delay += random.uniform(0, delay * 0.1). Make backoff configurable via EvalConfig. Document retry strategy in comments.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/config.py:1\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Hook adapter in hook_adapter.py does not sandbox hook execution\",\n      \"category\": \"security\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"high\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/hook_adapter.py\",\n      \"line\": 80,\n      \"line_range_end\": 100,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"def run_hook(self, hook_name: str, context: dict) -> dict:\\n    hook_path = self._hooks_dir / f\\\"{hook_name}.py\\\"\\n    if not hook_path.exists():\\n        return {}\\n    # Executes hook with full eval process privileges\\n    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(hook_name, hook_path)\\n    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\\n    spec.loader.exec_module(module)\\n    return module.run(context)\",\n      \"why\": \"Hooks are loaded and executed with the same privileges as the eval harness itself. A malicious or buggy hook can read/write arbitrary files, make network calls, exfiltrate data, or corrupt eval state. There's no sandboxing, capability restriction, or resource limit on hook execution.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Run hooks in a restricted subprocess with limited file access (only scratch_root + explicit allowlist), no network, and a timeout. Use seccomp or a lightweight sandbox (firejail, bubblewrap) if available. Validate hook signatures: must accept context dict, return dict. Log hook execution time and resource usage. Document hook security model.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/isolation.py:1\",\n        \"tests/cli/eval/test_hook_adapter.py:1\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Claude process wrapper in claude_process.py does not enforce memory limits\",\n      \"category\": \"resource-leak\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/claude_process.py\",\n      \"line\": 120,\n      \"line_range_end\": 140,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"def spawn_claude(self, spec: Spec) -> subprocess.Popen:\\n    cmd = [\\\"claude\\\", \\\"--spec\\\", str(spec.path)]\\n    return subprocess.Popen(\\n        cmd,\\n        stdout=subprocess.PIPE,\\n        stderr=subprocess.PIPE,\\n        # No memory limit enforcement\\n    )\",\n      \"why\": \"If a spec triggers a Claude process that allocates unbounded memory (e.g., loading a huge file into RAM), it can OOM-kill the host. subprocess.Popen does not enforce memory limits by default. The eval harness has disk_budget but no RAM budget.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Use resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_AS, (max_bytes, max_bytes)) in a preexec_fn before spawning. Alternatively, use cgroups (cgexec) to enforce memory limits if running on Linux. Add a memory_limit_mb field to EvalConfig. Document that memory limits require Linux or preexec_fn support.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/config.py:1\",\n        \"tests/cli/eval/test_claude_process.py:1\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Artifact layout in run_report.py uses hardcoded directory names without central registry\",\n      \"category\": \"architecture\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"low\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/run_report.py\",\n      \"line\": 60,\n      \"line_range_end\": 80,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"def _get_spec_output_dir(self, spec_name: str) -> Path:\\n    return self._run_output_dir / \\\"specs\\\" / spec_name\\n\\ndef _get_run_summary_path(self) -> Path:\\n    return self._run_output_dir / \\\"summary.json\\\"\",\n      \"why\": \"run_report.py and artifact_layout.py both define artifact paths (\\\"specs\\\", \\\"summary.json\\\", etc.) but there's no central registry. If a path changes, both files must be updated. This is duplication and a maintenance burden.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Create a single ArtifactPaths dataclass in artifact_la